# Joy to the World (repertoire plan: joy-to-the-world). Lowell Mason's tune Antioch (1839) on
# Isaac Watts's words (1719), in D, the whole tune for verses 1 and 2. The melody is the one
# independent recollections agreed on note for note, as the carol is commonly sung today (older
# printings vary), played an octave below the sung pitch so it sits in first position.
# Line 1 is the D major scale stepping down an octave: D (string 2 fret 3), C# (string 2 fret 2),
# B (open string 2), A (string 3 fret 2), G (open string 3), F# (string 4 fret 4), E (string 4
# fret 2), D (open string 4). Rhythm: D quarter, C# dotted eighth, B sixteenth | A dotted quarter,
# G eighth | F# E | D, then "Let" on the last eighth.
# Line 2 climbs back up in pairs, a long note and a short one on the same pitch: B B, C# C#, D D.
# Line 3 runs down from D twice in eighths. The refrain repeats F#, then E, four times and flicks
# up a step on a sixteenth; the last line leaps from low D to high D and walks home.
# One finger per fret in first position (finger = fret number). The teacher strums D, G and A7,
# two strums a bar; the chords change on beat 2 where the melody does.
title: Joy to the World
id: joy-to-the-world
composer: Lowell Mason (tune Antioch); words by Isaac Watts
source: Words 1719; tune Antioch published 1839, melody as commonly sung today; verses 1 and 2; arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (Watts died in 1748, Mason in 1872); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 3
tags: melody, major scale, dotted rhythm, sixteenth notes, christmas, carol, lyrics
tempo: 72
time: 2/4
key: D

